        "text": "Step 5: Simplify \\(8^{34} + 8^{38} \\mod 11\\) using properties of exponents and modular arithmetic. Notice that \\(8^2 \\equiv 64 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^3 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 72 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^4 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 48 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^5 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 4 \\equiv 32 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^6 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 10 \\equiv 80 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^7 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 24 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^8 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^9 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 40 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^{10} \\equiv 8 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 56 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\). Since \\(8^{10} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\), we can reduce the exponents modulo 10:\n\\[8^{34} \\equiv (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 4 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11},\\]\n\\[8^{38} \\equiv (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}.\\]"
