  "raw_text": "Step 1: First, we simplify \\(129^{34} + 96^{38}\\) modulo 11.\n\nStep 2: Calculate \\(129 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[129 \\div 11 = 11 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 129 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}.\\]\nSo, \\(129^{34} \\equiv 8^{34} \\pmod{11}\\).\n\nStep 3: Calculate \\(96 \\mod 11\\):\n\\[96 \\div 11 = 8 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\Rightarrow 96 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}.\\]\nSo, \\(96^{38} \\equiv 8^{38} \\pmod{11}\\).\n\nStep 4: Combine the results from steps 2 and 3:\n\\[129^{34} + 96^{38} \\equiv 8^{34} + 8^{38} \\pmod{11}.\\]\n\nStep 5: Simplify \\(8^{34} + 8^{38} \\mod 11\\) using properties of exponents and modular arithmetic. Notice that \\(8^2 \\equiv 64 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^3 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 72 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^4 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 48 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^5 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 4 \\equiv 32 \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^6 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 10 \\equiv 80 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^7 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 24 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^8 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^9 \\equiv 8 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 40 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{11}\\), and \\(8^{10} \\equiv 8 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 56 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\). Since \\(8^{10} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{11}\\), we can reduce the exponents modulo 10:\n\\[8^{34} \\equiv (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^4 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 4 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11},\\]\n\\[8^{38} \\equiv (8^{10})^3 \\cdot 8^8 \\equiv 1^3 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{11}.\\]\n\nStep 6: Add the simplified results:\n\\[8^{34} + 8^{38} \\equiv 4 + 5 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{11}.\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{9}<|im_end|>",
